.TH WPA 8
.SH NAME
wpa \- WPA2-PSK supplicant
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ip/wpa
[
.B -d
] [
.BI -s " essid"
]
[
.I interface
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Wpa
authenticates this machine to a WPA2 personal (pre-shared key) wireless
network and installs the resulting keys in the radio.
.PP
The radio does the rest by itself.
A fullMAC part such as the CYW43455 on a Raspberry Pi 3B+ scans,
authenticates and associates in its own firmware, and the kernel driver
publishes the result as an ordinary ethernet interface,
by default
.BR /net/ether1 .
What the firmware cannot do is prove the machine knows the network's
passphrase, because the passphrase is not the radio's to hold.
That proof is the four-way EAPOL handshake, and that is
.IR wpa .
.PP
.I Wpa
opens a conversation on
.IR interface ,
connects it to ethernet type
.BR 0x888e ,
writes
.BI essid " name"
to the conversation's
.B ctl
file if
.B -s
was given \(em quoted, so a name containing spaces survives the
kernel's ctl parser \(em
waits for the interface's
.B ifstats
to report
.BR "status: associated" ,
and then writes
.BI auth " ie"
\- the RSN information element naming WPA2 with CCMP and PSK
authentication.
From then on it reads EAPOL frames from the conversation's
.B data
file, answers them, and installs the pairwise and group keys on the same
.B ctl
file:
.IP
.EX
txkey \fIbssid\fP ccmp:\fIkey\fP@\fIiv\fP
rxkey\fIn\fP \fIbssid\fP ccmp:\fIkey\fP@\fIiv\fP
.EE
.PP
When the driver drops the association it closes the queue,
.I wpa
reads end of file, and the whole sequence begins again.
.PP
It begins again on a growing pause \(em a tenth of a second, then
doubling to a minute \(em and it says so on a slower schedule still:
the first line at once, then never again for twenty seconds, then at
twice the previous interval up to ten minutes.
A link that will not come up therefore costs about twenty lines an hour
rather than a hundred thousand, and the lines that survive carry how
many attempts have been made and over how long, which is what
distinguishes a supplicant that is still trying from one that is
wedged.
An association that installs a key and lasts a second clears that
history: the next drop is announced at once and retried at once.
This matters because the machine this runs on may have nothing but a
serial console, and a supplicant that fills it cannot be typed at.
.PP
The options are:
.TP
.B -d
Report each frame sent and received, and the derived master key.
For debugging only: it prints key material.
.TP
.BI -s " essid"
Set the network name, and write it to the interface.
Without it the name is read from the interface's
.B ifstats
and
.I wpa
fails if there is none, because the name is the salt of the key
derivation and a wrong one produces a wrong key silently.
.SH FILES
.TF /mnt/factotum/rpc
.TP
.B /net/ether1/clone
opened to obtain a conversation; the number read back names the
conversation's directory.
.TP
.B /net/ether1/ifstats
read for
.B essid:
and
.BR status: .
.TP
.B /net/ether1/\fIn\fB/ctl
written with
.BR essid ,
.BR auth ,
.B txkey
and
.BR rxkey .
.TP
.B /net/ether1/\fIn\fB/data
EAPOL frames, with their ethernet headers.
.TP
.B /mnt/factotum/rpc
the passphrase, as
.BR "proto=wpapsk role=client essid=\fIname\fP" .
.TP
.B /dev/random
the station nonce.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/ip/wpa.b
.br
.B /appl/lib/wpakey.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR factotum (4),
.IR wpakey (2)
.PP
The passphrase is never an argument and never a file in the tree.
Give it to
.IR factotum (4)
once:
.IP
.EX
echo 'key proto=wpapsk role=client essid=home !password=secret' \e
	>/mnt/factotum/ctl
.EE
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Every line begins
.BR "wpa: " .
.B associated; starting the four-way handshake
means the radio reported an association and the RSN element has been
written.
It is printed for the first association, after any wait long enough to
have been reported, and after a link that would not hold was mentioned
\(em not for every re-association, because a driver that grants one
instantly grants thousands.
.B "still waiting for the radio to associate (\fIt\fP)"
means
.B ifstats
has reported
.BR unassociated ,
.B connecting
or
.B unauthenticated
for
.I t
so far.
.B pairwise receive key installed
and
.B pairwise transmit key installed
mean the handshake completed;
.B group key \fIn\fP installed
follows.
.B bad MIC
means a frame's integrity check did not match; one on a noisy link is
unremarkable.
A wrong passphrase more often gives no diagnostic at all: the access
point rejects the second message silently and the handshake stops,
so a handshake that begins and never completes should be read as a
wrong passphrase before anything else.
.B link lost; re-associating
means the driver deassociated; it is printed once, when the link goes.
.B "the link will not hold: \fIn\fP attempts over \fIt\fP, still trying every \fId\fP"
replaces it when the association keeps being granted and keeps carrying
nothing.
Both numbers climbing across two such lines is a supplicant still at
work; neither climbing is one that has stopped.
.SH BUGS
Only WPA2 personal with CCMP is implemented.
Key descriptor versions 2 (HMAC-SHA1) and 3 (AES-128-CMAC) are both
handled; WPA1 and TKIP (version 1) are refused with a diagnostic naming
what was asked for.
WPA3/SAE, WPA enterprise (802.1X/EAP) and fast roaming are not
implemented at all.
.PP
Understanding the version 3 descriptor is not the same as supporting
protected management frames: the RSN element
.I wpa
offers advertises no such capability, so an access point that requires
them refuses the association before any of this runs.
.PP
The supplicant does not fork into the background; run it with
.BR & .
